# (file, heading line prefix, callout body). Each callout goes at the end of that section.
N = [
("05_gpcr", "### The trimeric G protein", "She writes: \"Ligand binding to GPCR induces a conformational change which in turn alters the conformation of the trimeric G protein. Alteration of the α-subunit of the G protein allows it to exchange GDP for GTP.\"\n\nPlain version: the receptor changes shape, that bends the G protein, and the bend lets Gα drop GDP and pick up GTP. \"Conformation\" just means shape. \"Heterotrimeric\" just means three different pieces."),
("05_gpcr", "#### Drugs that copy or block hormones", "She writes the drugs as letters: IP (isoproterenol), AP (alprenolol), EP (epinephrine), and says the agonist has a \"lower Kd than EP.\"\n\nPlain version: Kd is grip strength, and lower Kd means a tighter grip, like a lower golf score being better. So isoproterenol grips the receptor harder than epinephrine does."),
("05_gpcr", "#### Protein kinase A", "She writes: \"binding of the first cAMP molecule lowers the Kd for binding of the second cAMP.\"\n\nPlain version: once one cAMP is on, the second one sticks more easily. That's all \"cooperative\" means."),
("05_gpcr", "#### Glycogen:", "Her sentence chains three enzymes with almost the same name: \"phosphorylating and activating the enzyme glycogen phosphorylase kinase which then phosphorylates and activates glycogen phosphorylase which then degrades glycogen.\"\n\nPlain version: PKA → phosphorylase KINASE → phosphorylase → glycogen chopped up. Write it as an arrow chain and the sentence stops being confusing."),
("05_gpcr", "### Turning the cAMP signal off", "She asks \"What would happen if trimeric G proteins could not be down regulated?\" and writes \"Change in affinity of hormone for receptor when Gα is in GTP bound form.\"\n\nPlain version: \"could not be down regulated\" means the G protein can't switch off, so the answer is always \"the signal stays on.\" \"Change in affinity\" means the receptor's grip on the hormone gets weaker, so the hormone falls off. Her \"Consequence?\" next to cholera toxin is asking you to finish the chain: Gαs stuck on, cAMP high, water pours into the gut."),
("05_gpcr", "#### What calcium does", "Her calmodulin slide lists \"activation of phosphatases (e.g. cAMP phosphodiesterase).\"\n\nPlain version: strictly, PDE is a phosphodiesterase, not a phosphatase, but she groups it there. If she asks what Ca2+/calmodulin turns on, \"cAMP phosphodiesterase\" is in her list."),
("05_gpcr", "#### G proteins that open K+ channels", "She writes: \"Note that the signal subunit (and not Gα-GTP) is transduced via the Gβγ.\"\n\nPlain version: in this heart example, the Gβγ half is the one that opens the K+ channel. Gα isn't the messenger here."),
("05_gpcr", "### Vision:", "She writes that rods are \"active in the dark\" and that retinal \"isomerizes to the trans configuration.\"\n\nPlain version: \"active\" means releasing neurotransmitter, and light is what quiets them. \"Isomerizes to trans\" means the bent molecule straightens out (cis = bent, trans = straight)."),
("05_gpcr", "### Smell", "This slide is a figure pasted from a journal review, so the text is dense and runs into the article around it.\n\nPlain version, the one fact people miss: these neurons keep Cl- high inside, so when the Ca2+-activated Cl- channel opens, Cl- leaves and the cell gets MORE excited. That's the opposite of the usual \"Cl- calms a neuron\" rule."),
("06_synapse", "### The action potential", "She writes: \"a rapid, transient, all-or-none electrical activity propagated in the plasma membrane of excitable cells.\"\n\nPlain version: a quick, short spike that's either full size or not there, which travels along neurons and muscle cells. \"Transient\" = short-lived. \"Excitable cells\" = neurons and muscle."),
("06_synapse", "#### Why Na+ rushes in", "She writes: \"The inward movement of Na+ ions is energetically very favorable.\"\n\nPlain version: Na+ rolls downhill into the cell on its own, because both the crowding and the charge push it in."),
("06_synapse", "### Why action potentials only travel forward", "She asks: \"How is an action potential propagated unidirectionally??\"\n\nPlain version: \"Why does the spike only go one way?\" The answer she wants is the inactivated Na+ channels behind the spike, not myelin."),
("06_synapse", "### Myelin and saltatory conduction", "Two things on these slides read harder than they are. Her myelin slide leaves the two speeds blank (\"velocity... non-myelinated axon -\" and \"myelinated axon -\"), so fill them from class. And for Guillain-Barré she writes the host \"mounts an immune response to a foreign pathogen and instead the immune system attacks gangliosides.\"\n\nPlain version of that: you fight off an infection, and the immune system gets confused and attacks the insulation on your body's nerves."),
("06_synapse", "### The neuromuscular junction", "She writes \"transmitted gated cation channel.\"\n\nPlain version: that's a typo for transmitter-gated, which means the same as ligand-gated: a channel that opens when the neurotransmitter binds."),
("06_synapse", "#### Five channels from nerve to contraction", "She writes: \"the opening of acetylcholine-gated cation channels leads to an influx of Ca2+ in the muscle cell (involves 5 different ion channels).\"\n\nPlain version: this makes it sound like the ACh channel itself lets the calcium in. It mainly lets Na+ in. The big Ca2+ rise comes from the SR at the end of the five-channel chain. Read her sentence as \"opening the ACh channel starts a chain that ends with Ca2+ flooding the muscle.\""),
("06_synapse", "### Drugs and toxins at the synapse", "For Valium she writes: \"positive allosteric modulators... increases the total conduction of chloride ions... As a result, the difference between resting potential and threshold potential is increased.\"\n\nPlain version: Valium binds a different spot on the GABA receptor and helps GABA work, more Cl- comes in, and the neuron ends up further from its firing point."),
("07_opto", "### The discoveries, in order", "For ChR2 she writes: \"a broad, non-selective cation channel that efficiently conducts both monovalent and divalent positive ions.\"\n\nPlain version: it lets most plus ions through, both the single-charge ones (Na+, K+) and the double-charge one (Ca2+)."),
("07_opto", "### How channelrhodopsin-2 works", "She writes: \"covalently linked to the chromophore all trans-retinal. Upon absorption of blue light changes to the 13-cis-retinal conformation.\"\n\nPlain version: \"chromophore\" is the light-catching part. Watch the direction: in ChR2 it goes all-trans TO 13-cis, the reverse of the rod in Lecture 5 (11-cis to all-trans)."),
("07_opto", "### Getting it into a living animal", "She writes: \"By altering the genetic code of the virus, they could dictate exactly which types of neurons would produce the light-sensitive protein.\"\n\nPlain version: they picked a promoter (the gene's on-switch) that's only active in one neuron type. If asked how only certain neurons get ChR2, answer \"cell-type-specific promoter.\""),
("07_opto", "### Experiments she showed", "She writes \"The First In Vivo Proof (2007).\"\n\nPlain version: \"in vivo\" means in a living animal, as opposed to \"in culture\" (in a dish)."),
("07_opto", "### The optogenetic toolbox", "For OptoXR she writes: \"intracellular domains of rhodopsin have been engineered to activate different Galpha proteins and thus controlling intracellular signaling.\"\n\nPlain version: they swapped the inside loops so light switches on a G protein of their choice. That makes OptoXR the one optogenetic tool that is a GPCR."),
("08_rtk", "### How an RTK turns on", "Two naming slips on these slides. The FGF title says \"heparin sulfate\" while the text says \"heparan sulfate\"; treat them as the same thing (heparan sulfate is the right name). And the EGF slide is titled \"activation of the EFG receptor,\" a typo for EGF.\n\nShe also writes \"formation of an asymmetric kinase domain dimer.\" Plain version: the two kinase halves aren't mirror images; one switches the other on."),
("08_rtk", "### Turning RTKs off", "She writes: \"average half life on cell surface of ~10-15 hours\" and \"Covalent modification of receptors by mono-ubiquitinylation promote endocytosis.\"\n\nPlain version: a receptor normally lasts about 10-15 hours on the surface, and sticking one ubiquitin label on it gets it swallowed and digested."),
("08_rtk", "### Adaptors link RTKs to Ras", "Her answer to \"How could RTKs activate Ras???\" is just the slide title \"Adaptor proteins link RTKs to Ras.\"\n\nPlain version: on the exam, name them. GRB2 is the adaptor (SH2 to the receptor, SH3 to Sos) and Sos is the GEF that flips Ras on. \"Adaptor proteins\" alone may not get full marks."),
("08_rtk", "### Proving Ras works downstream", "She writes that the glycine 12 change \"blocks the functional binding of GAP proteins and in essence locks Ras in the active GTP-bound state.\"\n\nPlain version: GAP can't do its job, so Ras can't turn off. \"Constitutively active\" = always on. \"Dominant negative\" sounds strong, but it's a blocker: a broken protein that jams the normal one."),
("08_rtk", "### The fly eye", "She writes \"Signaling from R8 is necessary to induce R7 fate\" and calls Boss \"a seven pass transmembrane receptor protein\" on R8.\n\nPlain version: \"induce R7 fate\" = make the cell become R7. Boss is the ligand here (it activates Sevenless) even though she calls it a receptor protein; it's the 7-pass protein sitting on R8."),
("08_rtk", "### MAPK turns on genes", "She writes \"quiescent cultured mammalian cells\" and uses TCF for \"ternary complex factor.\"\n\nPlain version: \"quiescent\" = resting, not dividing. And this TCF is NOT the Wnt TCF in Lecture 11 (T-cell factor). Same three letters, different protein."),
("08_rtk", "#### The retinotopic map", "She writes: \"The preference for posterior neurons to avoid posterior tectum is not due to the attractive nature of anterior tectum, but a repulsive factor on posterior tectum.\"\n\nPlain version: the back of the tectum pushes these axons away. Nothing is pulling them to the front."),
("08_rtk", "#### Ephrin and Eph receptors", "This slide is a figure pasted from a review article, and the text from the article runs into her labels, so it reads as a jumble.\n\nPlain version, all you need from it: ephrinA = GPI-anchored, binds EphA (8 members). EphrinB = transmembrane with a tail inside, binds EphB (5 members), and can signal back into its own cell (reverse signaling). 13 Eph receptors in humans."),
("09_cancer1", "### Two defining properties", "She writes \"Avoid replicative cell senescence.\"\n\nPlain version: cancer cells never run out of divisions (because they keep telomerase on)."),
("09_cancer1", "### Tumors: benign vs malignant", "She writes that benign tumors are only a problem if they \"secrete excess amounts of biologically active substances,\" and defines a tumor as cells that proliferate \"in defiance of normal restraints on cell division.\"\n\nPlain version: benign tumors matter if they get big or make too much of a hormone or similar. A tumor is cells dividing while ignoring the stop signals."),
("09_cancer1", "### New metabolites", "She writes \"70% of brain cancers... have a mutation in isocitrate dehydrogenase.\"\n\nPlain version: the real figure applies to certain brain cancers (lower-grade gliomas), not all of them. On her exam, though, \"70%\" with \"brain cancers\" is what she expects."),
("09_cancer1", "### The multi-hit model", "Her Lecture 9 colon figure labels the chromosome 18 step \"loss of tumor suppressor in DCC region.\" Her Lecture 11 figure renames the same step \"loss of Smad4 and other tumor suppressors.\"\n\nPlain version: same step, two names. If you see \"DCC region,\" think Smad4 on chromosome 18."),
("09_cancer1", "### Metastasis", "She uses \"basement membrane\" on the metastasis slides and \"basal lamina\" elsewhere.\n\nPlain version: they're the same thin floor mat under the epithelium."),
("09_cancer1", "### Hypoxia, HIF and VEGF", "For VHL syndrome she writes that cells \"produce large quantities of HIF regardless of O2 levels\" (and spells it \"Hippel-Landau\").\n\nPlain version: HIF isn't being made faster. It just isn't being destroyed, because VHL (the garbage collector) is broken. The name is von Hippel-Lindau."),
("10_cancer2", "### Mutagen vs carcinogen", "Her summary line, \"All genotoxic carcinogens are mutagens, but not all mutagens are carcinogens, and not all carcinogens are mutagens,\" packs three claims into one sentence.\n\nPlain version: draw two overlapping circles. The overlap (genotoxic carcinogens) is in both. Each circle also has a part outside the other: mutagens that don't cause cancer, and carcinogens that don't touch DNA."),
("10_cancer2", "### Indirect carcinogens", "She writes: \"this oxidation process creates a highly unstable, electron-deficient intermediate, an electrophilic radical... It immediately attacks the electron-rich bases of nearby DNA.\"\n\nPlain version: the liver's cleanup step makes a molecule that's desperate for electrons, and it grabs them from DNA, which damages it."),
("10_cancer2", "### Non-genotoxic carcinogens", "Her table calls TPA a \"Receptor Mimic.\"\n\nPlain version: TPA mimics DAG, the second messenger that switches on PKC. If asked what it copies, say DAG. \"Forcing pre-mutated cells into rapid clonal expansion\" just means making already-mutated cells multiply fast."),
("10_cancer2", "### Bcr-Abl", "She writes: \"Bcr-Abl chimeric oncoprotein is constitutively active and able to phosphorylate signal transduction molecules that are not normal substrates for c-Abl.\"\n\nPlain version: Bcr-Abl is a two-part fused protein that's always on, and it tags proteins normal Abl never touches (like JAK2 and STAT5). \"Hematopoietic\" on the same slide means blood-forming."),
("10_cancer2", "### Cytokine receptors and the JAK-STAT", "She writes: \"Cytokines are stably associated with the cytoplasmic tyrosine kinases called Janus Kinases (JAKs).\"\n\nPlain version: she means the cytokine RECEPTORS are stuck to JAKs. The cytokine is the signal outside the cell; the JAK is attached to the receptor's tail inside."),
("11_cancer3", "### Part 5:", "She writes \"Five main classes of proteins encoded by tumor suppressor proteins.\"\n\nPlain version: she means tumor suppressor GENES. Proteins don't encode anything; genes do."),
("11_cancer3", "#### The pathway: think", "Two lines on the Wnt slides read harder than they are. She writes that β-catenin \"displaces the co-receptor Groucho,\" and that Dishevelled \"recruits axin. This leads to the disassembly of the destruction complex.\"\n\nPlain version: Groucho is a co-REPRESSOR (it keeps genes off), not a co-receptor; if she writes co-receptor she still means Groucho. And \"recruits axin\" sounds like Dishevelled builds the gang, but pulling axin away is what breaks the gang apart."),
("11_cancer3", "#### The discovery paradox", "She writes that in later stages TGFβ \"functions as a proto-oncogene and promotes oncogenesis.\"\n\nPlain version: she means it acts like an oncogene (it helps the cancer). Strictly, a proto-oncogene is the normal, healthy version of a gene, so the word is used loosely here. Also, \"may other family members prevent proliferation\" on that slide is a typo for \"many.\""),
("11_cancer3", "#### How the signal travels", "She writes that the Smad complex \"translocates to the nucleus.\"\n\nPlain version: \"translocates\" here just means moves into the nucleus. It's not a chromosome swap like the Philadelphia translocation in Lecture 9."),
]
